[
  {
    "sha": "dc3a06f132d184ff455416899995b6f8da46bc96",
    "node_id": "C_kwDOVoU8JdoAKGRjM2EwNmYxMzJkMTg0ZmY0NTU0MTY4OTk5OTViNmY4ZGE0NmJjOTY",
    "commit": {
      "author": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T09:52:36Z"
      },
      "committer": {
        "name": "Mike Kinney",
        "email": "mike.kinney@gmail.com",
        "date": "2026-11-12T09:52:36Z"
      },
      "message": "feat(edm): wire tabs and the tab cut-off - the slug held until it is freed, an unheld slug refused\n\nTabs are off by default (#290 D6). When on, the count, the width, one\nposition per tab and the cut-off's condition are all stated; each\nmissing or impossible one is refused by name (width at or past the\nside, a tab on a round's facets, overlapping tabs, a stretch the start\nhole cannot reach, a cut-off condition on another kerf offset). The\nmain cut leaves the tabs (each run entered by its own lead, left back\nalong its own kerf); the cut-off follows as its own section with its\nown condition after a program stop.\n\nThe hold law completes #534's slug law: a tabbed main cut must free\nnothing and its cut-off frees the stated piece; an untabbed cut whose\nslug drops is refused unless the op states a capture.\n\nFixes #539\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
